11.3  Certificates: how HTTPS proves who it is

TL;DR
  - A certificate says "this public key belongs to example.com", signed by a
    certificate authority (CA). Your system trusts 100 to 150 root CAs.
  - During the TLS handshake the server sends its certificate and the
    intermediates; your client checks the chain up to a trusted root, the name,
    and the dates.
  - Let's Encrypt gives free certificates that renew automatically; their
    lifetime is short, and getting shorter across the industry.

      openssl s_client -connect example.com:443 </dev/null   # chain, dates
      openssl x509 -in cert.pem -noout -subject -issuer -dates

On your machine
  Trusted   3 certificate authorities, in /etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt
  AES       aes-aesni, with the CPU's AES instructions · SHA-256: sha256-ni
  RNG       hardware generator tpm-rng-0 feeds the kernel
  TPM       version 2: can hold keys and seal disk keys

Pitfalls
  - "unable to get local issuer certificate": the server doesn't send its
    intermediate certificate, or the CA is a private one you must add.
  - Adding a company CA: copy it to /usr/local/share/ca-certificates/ and run
    sudo update-ca-certificates (Debian, Ubuntu), or
    /etc/pki/ca-trust/source/anchors/ and update-ca-trust (Fedora).
  - Python's requests and Node.js often use their own CA list, not the system's:
    REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE, NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS.
  - curl -k turns all checking off: it hides the problem and invites a man in
    the middle.

Deep dive

  ▾ Why trust a chain?

    Root CAs keep their keys offline and sign a few intermediate CAs, which sign
    servers' certificates. Your client only stores the roots; each link's
    signature proves the next was approved by the one above. Every certificate
    issued is also logged publicly (Certificate Transparency), so a CA issuing
    one for your domain behind your back gets noticed.

  ▾ What does a CA check before signing?

    That the requester controls the domain: by serving a token on the site over
    HTTP, or creating a DNS record (the ACME protocol automates it). Basic
    certificates prove nothing more, not who runs the site, just that you're
    talking to whoever controls the name.

Related
  Commands  openssl, curl, update-ca-certificates, update-ca-trust
  Paths     /etc/ssl/certs/, /etc/pki/tls/certs/,
            /usr/local/share/ca-certificates/
  See also  atfq 6.2 What happens when you open a website
  See also  atfq 11.2 Public-key cryptography
  See also  atfq 10.2 Keeping the clock right: NTP
